{
  "cases": [
    {
      "id": "C1",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §§3, 9.3 and 10.5 distinguish exact-grid scoring, repeatability evidence and the narrow intended interpretation. One run supports a reported performance result; repeatability remains unassessed in the supplied material.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "repeat_test",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "The report does not say whether runs were repeated.",
          "reason": "Execution history is missing."
        },
        {
          "id": "stable",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "70% in one reported run",
          "reason": "One score cannot establish consistency across runs."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C2",
      "instrument_review": "Under EFPA §9.3, the explicit single execution establishes that repeat testing was not performed. It establishes neither stability nor instability; the stated grid-performance use remains narrower than a general ability claim.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "repeat_test",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "ran the evaluation exactly once and did not repeat it",
          "reason": "The developers explicitly excluded repeat evaluations."
        },
        {
          "id": "stable",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "ran the evaluation exactly once",
          "reason": "A single execution supplies no evidence of repeated-run stability."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C3",
      "instrument_review": "Reinterpreting EFPA §9.3 as repeated evaluation of a fixed AI configuration, the controlled repetitions support stability under the report's own rule. This is local precision evidence, not an EFPA reliability coefficient or evidence of broader validity.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "repeat_test",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "repeated the evaluation ten times with the same system, inputs, instructions, resources, and settings",
          "reason": "Repeated evaluations held the stated conditions constant."
        },
        {
          "id": "stable",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "Scores ranged from 69% to 71%.",
          "reason": "The two-percentage-point range meets the stated maximum of two."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C4",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §9.3 separates conducting a precision study from obtaining satisfactory precision. Repetitions were controlled, but their score range fails the supplied stability rule; this limits confidence in individual-run comparisons.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "repeat_test",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "repeated the evaluation ten times with the same system, inputs, instructions, resources, and settings",
          "reason": "The passage explicitly describes controlled repetitions."
        },
        {
          "id": "stable",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "Scores ranged from 20% to 90%.",
          "reason": "The range exceeds the stated stability limit of two percentage points."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C5",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §6.3.8 treats installation guidance separately from §9 reliability evidence. The added operational documentation provides no new basis for judging repeated-run stability.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "repeat_test",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "The report does not say whether runs were repeated.",
          "reason": "Installation guidance does not resolve the missing execution history."
        },
        {
          "id": "stable",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "They report no additional evaluation results.",
          "reason": "No repeated-run results establish stability."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C6",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §§2.2–2.5 and 10.5 require matching score interpretation to intended use. The observed comparison supports the stated grid-puzzle use; cross-domain intelligence is not established.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "narrow",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "System A solves 80 puzzles and system B solves 60",
          "reason": "A solved more puzzles in the reported evaluation."
        },
        {
          "id": "broad",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "Only grid puzzles were tested; no findings about other tasks are supplied.",
          "reason": "The evidence does not establish intelligence across domains."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C7",
      "instrument_review": "Under EFPA §§6.1.1, 10.1 and 10.5, the broad intended use requires evidence extending beyond the tested grid domain. The stated ambition does not validate that interpretation, although the local performance difference is supported.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "narrow",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "System A solves 80 puzzles and system B solves 60",
          "reason": "A performed better on the evaluated puzzles."
        },
        {
          "id": "broad",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "Only grid puzzles were tested; no findings about other tasks are supplied.",
          "reason": "A broad intended use does not supply cross-domain evidence."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C8",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §§10.3.1.6, 10.3.2 and 10.5 distinguish benchmark scores from evidence about practical outcomes. Random allocation, blinded correctness checking and reported uncertainty support the workshop correctness comparison. Safety, time and generalisation beyond the studied conditions remain unestablished.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "before",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "Initially, only test scores were available.",
          "reason": "Those scores alone do not establish an improvement in workshop decisions."
        },
        {
          "id": "after",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "With A, 90% of decisions were correct; with B, 70%.",
          "reason": "The randomised trial supports better correctness with A under the studied conditions; the difference interval also excludes zero."
        },
        {
          "id": "safety",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "Time and safety outcomes were not measured.",
          "reason": "Correctness findings do not establish an unmeasured safety benefit."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P1A",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §§6.2.6 and 9.3 identify missing precision documentation. A headline score without execution history establishes neither a repeat study nor instability.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "gives no execution history",
          "reason": "The passage does not report whether repetitions occurred."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "one headline score of 75%",
          "reason": "A single reported score cannot demonstrate variability across evaluations."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P1B",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §9.3 treats absent repetitions as missing stability evidence, not evidence of unstable scores. The execution record explicitly rules out a repeat study.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "no repeat evaluations were conducted",
          "reason": "The execution record explicitly denies repetitions."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "this was the only evaluation performed",
          "reason": "Without repeated scores, instability is not demonstrated."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P2A",
      "instrument_review": "Under the AI reinterpretation of EFPA §9.3, the six controlled executions provide local repeatability evidence. Their range meets the stated rule; this does not establish precision for other configurations or tasks.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "identical inputs, settings and resources on six occasions",
          "reason": "The same AI and problems were evaluated repeatedly under the stated fixed conditions."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "73, 74, 74, 75, 74 and 73 percent",
          "reason": "The two-percentage-point range is within the three-point maximum."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P2B",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §9.3 distinguishes a repeatability investigation from satisfactory results. The controlled repetitions provide direct evidence of failure under the report's stability rule.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "identical inputs, settings and resources on six occasions",
          "reason": "Repeated evaluations used the same stated conditions."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "51, 66, 79, 55, 82 and 62 percent",
          "reason": "The score range exceeds the permitted three percentage points."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P3A",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §§6.2, 6.1.3.2–6.1.3.3 and 9 distinguish descriptive documentation, item development evidence and precision evidence. Topic and scoring descriptions establish neither repeated testing nor answer errors.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "It gives no execution history",
          "reason": "Repeated evaluations are not documented."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "no findings about mistakes in the provided answers",
          "reason": "No findings establish answer errors or their absence."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P3B",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §6.3.8 recognises the appendix as operational guidance. It adds no evidence for §9 repeatability or §§6.1 and 7 item-answer quality; missing evidence remains distinct from demonstrated defects.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "It gives no execution history",
          "reason": "The technical appendix does not establish repeated evaluations."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "It reports no further evaluation results or examination of answers.",
          "reason": "The appendix supplies no evidence of answer mistakes."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P4A",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §§6.1.3.2–6.1.3.3 recognise independent editorial examination as development evidence. It does not prove released-answer correctness. Agreement before reconciliation is unreported; editorial agreement would also differ from scoring inter-rater reliability under §9.6.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "Two editors independently examined every question and supplied answer before release.",
          "reason": "Both questions and answers underwent development review."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "does not state whether mistakes were found or how many remained",
          "reason": "Review procedures alone do not establish that every released answer is correct."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P4B",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §§6.1.3 and 7 separate review procedures from material quality. Independent examination occurred, but the identified released-answer errors are direct adverse evidence. Unreported pre-reconciliation agreement cannot be inferred.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "Two editors independently examined every question and supplied answer before release.",
          "reason": "The stated development process examined both."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "twelve supplied answers as wrong in the released version",
          "reason": "The passage explicitly identifies incorrect released answers."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P5A",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §§3 and 6.3.2 distinguish deterministic scoring from scoring correctness. Under §9.6, inter-rater reliability is inapplicable to the described fixed, nonjudgemental marking rule; independent correctness validation remains undocumented.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "No comparison of those marks with independently established correct marks is reported.",
          "reason": "An unreported comparison cannot be assumed to have occurred."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "identical text always receives the same mark",
          "reason": "Consistency of marking does not establish correctness for every response."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P5B",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §6.3.2 makes the independent scoring check relevant evidence. Its unresolved discrepancies show that deterministic execution does not ensure correctness. The 20 chosen responses also cannot establish universal accuracy.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "tested the software against independently established correct marks for 20 chosen responses",
          "reason": "The passage explicitly reports an independent-reference comparison."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "Two marks differed and the discrepancies remain unresolved.",
          "reason": "Observed disagreements with established correct marks conflict with universal scoring correctness."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P6A",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §10.5 supports the tightly bounded intended interpretation from the reported counts. Section 8.3.2.2 permits a norms exception for a solely raw-score ranking purpose; no population-standard interpretation is needed for this comparison.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "identifying which system solved more of these 48 grid problems in this evaluation",
          "reason": "The reported solution counts directly answer this limited question."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "A solves 36 and B solves 24.",
          "reason": "A solved more evaluated problems under the fixed conditions."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P6B",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §§10.1 and 10.5 require validity evidence appropriate to the broad intended interpretation. The local counts establish the grid comparison, while general intelligence across subjects and settings remains unestablished.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "No results from other tasks or settings are supplied.",
          "reason": "The evidence does not establish sufficiency for the stated cross-domain use."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "A solves 36 and B solves 24.",
          "reason": "The reported counts directly establish the local performance difference."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P7A",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §§3.2 and 6.1.3.1 assess scoring against the measurement aim. Exact-grid scoring aligns with the explicitly restricted requirement; withholding partial credit is not a defect relative to that requirement.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "count only completely correct output grids",
          "reason": "The all-or-nothing rule implements the stated requirement."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "partial correctness is deliberately outside this comparison",
          "reason": "Zero credit for partial grids follows the requirement."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P7B",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §§3.2, 6.1.3.1 and 6.3.2 expose an explicit mismatch between the scoring aim and implemented rule. All-or-nothing scoring does not implement the required cell-level credit.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "give a response credit for every correctly completed cell, including partially correct output grids",
          "reason": "Assigning zero to partial grids conflicts with the specified credit requirement."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "A partially correct grid receives zero.",
          "reason": "The rule withholds the cell-level credit explicitly required for partial grids."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P8A",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §§2.8, 6.2.4 and 9 distinguish fixed-condition repetition from sensitivity to changed administration. This study examines instruction wording changes, not identical-condition repeatability; the magnitude and precision of the effect are unreported.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "No evaluation repeats an identical instruction.",
          "reason": "Instructions differ, so conditions are not identical."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "each evaluation uses a differently worded instruction",
          "reason": "The study reports score changes while varying instructions and holding the other stated conditions fixed."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P8B",
      "instrument_review": "Under EFPA §9.3, this is a fixed-condition repeatability study. It does not test sensitivity to instruction changes, and the absence of numerical results prevents judging whether its scores are stable.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "software, resources, problem order, settings and instruction are identical on every evaluation",
          "reason": "The same AI and problems are evaluated with the stated conditions held constant."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "No different instruction or presentation is studied.",
          "reason": "The passage explicitly excludes an instruction-change comparison."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "closing_note": "No task instruction required clarification. The original EFPA model guided these focused reviews; no substitute instrument, aggregate score or EFPA endorsement is implied. Part A descriptions and Part B judgements use only each passage, independently; Part C sources are limited to the supplied material. Test taker is reinterpreted as the evaluated AI configuration, administration as evaluation conditions, and retest as repeated execution. Human identity, sensory and motor prerequisites, disability accommodations, professional purchasing qualifications and personal debriefing are inapplicable to the AI as test taker; fairness affecting human users or stakeholders remains potentially relevant but unestablished. Other unspecified domains, including alternate forms, IRT, dimensionality, fairness and generated-report quality, remain unassessed rather than presumed defective. Published result cards are not automatically interpreted as EFPA digitally generated individual reports. No EFPA numerical ratings were assigned: where applicable evidence is insufficient, its original category would be 0, not 1; n/a requires actual inapplicability. Any critical 0 or 1 would trigger the original restricted-use guidance, without proving poor underlying quality. The supplied extraction contains apparent numbering and anchor inconsistencies; none affects these claim classifications. Instrument attribution: EFPA (2025), Test Review Model, version 5.0, https://www.efpa.eu/, CC BY-SA 4.0; AI reinterpretations are identified above."
}
